Z||000005||theogre||04-25-2001||07:49 PM||||It get's better. <P>In more and more states seatbelts are a "primary offence." They can stop you for no other reason than they can't see you wearing it. (In most states you had to be stopped for something else and then seatbelt get's tacked on....)<P>Have someone sit in you Fiero and put on the seat belt.<P>Then walk back 50-60 feet behind the drivers seat and look at the car. Can you tell for sure if the belt is on or not?<P>Now move 10 feet to the left( aprox distance of one lane of roadway.) Can you even see the driver now?<P>Walk parallel up along side of the car and see how far you get before you can tell for sure the seatbelt is on....<P>Now keep in mind that most of you trying the above experiment are viewing standing up.... Lower your eye level to that of a seated driver in another car and repeat the experiment.... <P>Seatbelt laws are becoming just another way to profile people who drive certain cars and/or based on race.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||JSocha||04-25-2001||11:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>Whether you like seatbelts or not, it is not the gov't's business to tell you that you HAVE to wear them.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Guys, you really have to stop and think about this one for a moment.<P>The Gov is smarter then we give them credit for because they themselves realizes that if we are dead, they can not tax us anymore (other then perhaps a final Estate Tax)!<P>IE, No taxes, no paycheck w/bonuses and perks.<P>Therefore, make a law that we can now legally enforce upon John Q. Public to get what we can before they die and make it sound like we are doing it for JQ Public's benefit as well as safety as the "selling" point.<P>In the meantime guys, I'm looking for an email of Government facts that I received recently to post.<P>I hate seatbelts and do not wear them myself anymore, especially after a car accident I had back in 91 with my 88 LeBaron.<P>Now, I do not know what the enforcement rules are from state to state, but if it is required by law in most states (ie like it is here in ND), why then is it not enforced on school buses?  <P>I asked my step-daughters if they have to wear seatbelts on the bus.  They asked, "what seat belts?"<P>Thus my answer:  When was the last time your child paid taxes?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jnjsochasig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||04-26-2001||12:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by My7Fieros:<BR><B>[b]O M G</B> what a senseless gripe....<BR>"not violating someones rights"? c'mon...<BR>Its not like you can get arrested for driving your car. Simply put your seatbelt on! Like its some big pain in the ass...you should be doing it anyway!<BR>"violating rights"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is your opinion and you are entitled to it... "You should be doing it anyway." Who decided that? As I said before, will you wear the flame suit when someone decides that you should be doing that too?<P>And when you get cut in half running under a semi or mangled some other way in your car <I>because</I> you had your seat belt on, you might think it is a pain in the ass, or even more likely <B>your choice</B>.<P>You nailed it, JSocha. Like I said in my post, it's all about the money. And the gov't (and the lobbies) will always put out enough propoganda to fool some of the bleeding hearts in the country who will follow like sheep, or is that Lemmings in the computer world. Amazing that our own gov't doesn't give us credit for enough intelligence to decide whether or not we want to wear a seatbelt! And when we don't like it, it is a senseless gripe. <P>Not too senseless to me. I've paid tickets for no seatbelts three times. And you know what, I'll keep paying them because I will not wear one. I think I am old enough to make that decision...<P>Will it be a senseless gripe too when the law comes busting into my house and arrest me if am not using a condom?!? heheh<P>I am putting my life in danger by not doing so, right? Well, actually no, since I am in a monogamous relationship, but you get the idea.... It is all about our right to choose. Sorry that some people don't see that we should have that right. Just be careful of that first step at the cliff, it's a doozie!<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||frontal lobe||04-26-2001||12:22 AM||||WHEN will you guys realize that politicians try to get into government because they are SO much smarter than you, and know better for you, and so just want to take care of you?  So come on, wear your seatbelt like a good little boy or I'm going to have to make you sit in your room for a half an hour.<P>Actually, I am for wearing seat belts.  However, as long as someone is willing to take responsibility for themselves, I am against laws enforcing it.  Sticky issue is that kids riding in the car really can't take on that responsibility themselves and are dependent on the parents.<P>I got pulled over once on an interstate because I passed a state trooper (I was creeping by him at about 57 mph in a 55 when he was going 55) because he THOUGHT he saw one of my daughters not wearing a seat belt (she was).  But I'm sure he was proud of himself anyway for THINKING he was protecting a citizen.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Songman||04-26-2001||01:20 AM||||Children are a different story. As you say, they are not big enough and old enough to take responsibility for themselves. They are the responsibility of their parents. Still not the government. I agree that children should be buckled up in the back seat. But I am not going to tell my neighbor that he is a bad man for not buckling up his child, any more than I would tell him how to talk to his child about sex or drugs.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||04-26-2001||06:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by My7Fieros:<BR><B>[b]O M G</B> what a senseless gripe....<BR>"not violating someones rights"? c'mon...<BR>Its not like you can get arrested for driving your car. Simply put your seatbelt on! Like its some big pain in the ass...you should be doing it anyway!<BR>"violating rights"   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Tell that to the texas woman. She was pulled over for JUST not wearing her belt, yes her kid wasnt either. They arrested her, took her kid, impounded her truck, booked mug shot and fingerprinted her, jailed her. She had to have cash bond posted, pay to get her truck back, hoping the tow truck didnt screw it up, then find her kid. I personally think that was overkill. They could have given her a ticket. Its all about the MONEY and being God over us all. I wear my belt and sometimes I dont...im old enough to decide myself...i dont need someone to tell me what to do.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||SteveJ||04-28-2001||11:18 AM||||Did no one else read the part of the article where she had NO ID and NO INSURANCE CARD ??????<P>Now in my concept of the world a person not supplying ID (driver's license) wins a free trip to the station.<P>Ask yourself, did the cop who pulled over Tim McVeigh do the right thing?<p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 04-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||04-28-2001||01:37 PM||||I was just repeating what they said on the news. nothing was mentioned about the ID or Insurance. Leave it to media.....that puts a different spin on the whole thing Steve.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Mach10||04-28-2001||06:22 PM||||First off, I'm a little disapointed at the lack of support in this motion. I will wade into the fray, and bring forth the following, for your flammable enjoyment    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>I live in Manitoba. Seatbelts are mandatory, unless the car does not have them (before '76?) We get fines if caught w/o belts. I heartily approve. Seatbelts DO save lives. In the case of an intrusion into the passenger compartment, there is not much that will save you. You will be better off stuck in your seat. The whole point of the seatbelt is to allow your body to absorb the impact of the crash predictably, and dissipate the force along strong and non-critical areas e.g., the strong, flexible ribcage and shoulders, and the well-boned hip.<P>When you aren't buckled in, you go forward, into the steering column, then the dash (skip step one for psgr) then out the windsheild, then into the OTHER PERSONS CAR, (Or alternatively, meet the other driver on his way into your car...) <P>As for:<BR>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I asked my step-daughters if they have to wear seatbelts on the bus. They asked, "what seat belts?"<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Uh, you are talking about two classes of vehicles. Take even a full-sized SUV, and run it head-on into a schoolbus. I guarantee you that there won't be much left of the SUV, but that the schooldbus WILL be still recognizeable. Moreover, the schoolbus will STILL BE MOVING FORWARD. The sudden slow-down may throw some children forward off the seats, but a couple of bitten tongues would not be considered much of an injury. As for the occupants of the SUV, At least they'll have lots of little kids to send Lilacs to the family(ies). When crash-tests were performed on school busses (most busses fall into the same category) it was found that even in a serious crash, injuries would be kept lower by not including seatbelts simply due to the massiveness of the bus compared to most everything on the road. The seats were re-designed to be padded, since in a serious crash, the occupants thrown forward would not recieve spinal and head injuries that they would have while buckled in, since only lap-belts could be used. 3-points would be great, but far too difficult [expensive] to implement. This was decided based on the likelyhood of different types of crashes. Obviously, in a roll-over situation, belts would be prefered, but since vehicle-vehicle accidents were more common in this class, provisions were made to decrease injury on the more "popular" accidents.<P>Maintaining that "seatbelts are bad[m'kay]" simply because you had one accident where you survived w/o a seatbelt, or sustained injuries as a result of a seatbelt is silly. In logical terms, it is a fallacy of reasoning, similar to this argument:<P>Given:<BR>1) I am 5 times more likely to die in a car-crash than in an airplane.<BR>2) I want to go to the store, down the street.<BR>3) I don't want to die<BR>Then: My best chance of getting back would be to fly to the store. So I'll fly down the street to 7-11.<P>Sounds goofy, huh?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Well, it's the same kind of reasoning, by taking things out of context. It has been proven, both statistically, and empiricly(sp?) that seatbelts reduce the severity of injuries. in <B>most</B> kinds of accidents. I will grant, then, that there are certain types of accidents that a seatbelt will not help, indeed, may make it worse. <P>Take your example of the car-intrusion by a semi. Good example. BUT, how often do you think you will rear-end a semi, at speed, compared to being smoked by some drunk *******  running a red light, or some person spinning out in front of you, coming the opposite way? To get past the safety bar present in all trailers (up here, they are mandatory) you have to hit fairly fast. Not likely in a normal accident situation.<P>Basically, what you are doing, is bypassing a safety system designed to work in MOST CASES in order to protect yourself from a WORST CASE. If you know that you will be in 100 crashes, but only 5 or 6  will be with a parked semi, on the highway, wouldn't it be nice to survive the other 95 first?<BR>*whew*<P>Ok, I'll grant that sending someone to jail may be a little harsh. But this way, everyone will be wearing them, if nothing else, out of fear. <P>One thing I have noticed about American culture is it's over-emphasis on Rights and Freedoms. Freedom is overrated. Don't get me started    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Having stated my stance on this kind of issue, I should point out my ignorance of US law at this point. Did the SC's judgement mean that it is a Jailable offense, not to wear a seatbelt, or is it just that if the cop needs a reason to haul you in, he can use that 'excuse' (i.e., you are obviously doped-out, but he can't smell anything, and wants a blood-test)<P>***ADDED***<BR>I checked with nhtsa, and found that 12% of ALL accidents involve large trucks, which raises the bar a little. Replace my (5 to 6)/100 argument with 12/100. I still have yet to find data telling me how many of this 12% involved both a larger truck and a psgr car/light truck-van...<P>**ADDED**<BR>Ugh, next page of the document, I found statistics stating that there is a 2.01:1 ratio of single-vehicle accidents vs. multi-vehicle accident. This means that for every 100 trucks that wreck, only 50 actually involve any other vehicles. This drops the percent back down to about 5,6/100 accidents. Isn't this fun?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 04-28-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 04-28-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000014||Songman||04-28-2001||10:57 PM||||All of the above may very well be true... that is not what we are saying. The point we are trying to make is that it is not the governments place to make the decision for us. <P>And history has shown us how it only takes a little intrusion into your rights before you have none left. Oh my, some group just decide that skydiving is unsafe. The gov't bans skydiving... Of course, that is a little far fetched... or is it? They have to understand that we are not children. The world is a dangerous place but we make our decisions every day and take the chances. That is called life.<P>Every time this seatbelt thing comes up, it always gets turned to whether or not seatbelts are a good thing... that is not the point at all.... maybe someday we can get to the real point.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||04-28-2001||11:16 PM||||I know i'm going to regret this... Ok, now that the issue of seatbelt safety is dealt with, I guess I have to address the rest of the argument *sigh*<P>*dons asbestos jumpsuit*<P>If I was a government agency (hive mind  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), and was doing my job properly, and noticed that, yes, my citizens were takin the freedom that they had, and abusing themselves with it, I'd sure as hell take them away. Take the right to bear arms (I'm baring my arms now, it's warm out!). I see kids taking guns to school, and people carrying in the streets. Gun control or not, but hasn't it gotten out of hand? As soon as a government agency tries to control something as flammable as Gun Control, the entire nation rallies to the point of "It's our right" and "Only criminals will carry guns then." No-one seems to look at the big picture: Make guns VERY hard to comeby, and there will be less of them. I agree that some people should be <B>**ALLOWED**</B> own a gun, but there is no reason why just about anyone should be entitled. <BR>My supervisor at work here, has the saying "don't want to be treated like a child? Then prove to me that you are not a child [somewhat modified]" I agree that the argument is circular, but the point rests.<BR>Did you know that the US has a reputation world-wide of being a country full of conspiracy theorists? It's sad when a country distrusts it's own government(incidently, I find that very amusing... after all, you voted for them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).<P>While on the subject, it's the government's JOB to regulate hazardous activities. Case in point: drugs. True, some are more pathological than others, but they are still a threat to well-being. Funny, no-one seems to be screaming for the legalizing of Cocaine or Heroine. Funny, how not wearing a seat-belt will kill you faster than a life-long addiction... While I believe that jail-time is counter-productive in 95% of cases, I do believe in harsher punishment (PLEASE! Don't get me started on corporeal punishment  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). If it was me, I'd only deal harshly with parents that don't buckle their kids in. Otherwise, it's kinda comforting that the government is out for your well-being. Then again, I haven't had much reason to mistrust my government, and I haven't seen much reason to mistrust yours....<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000016||Tina||04-29-2001||12:13 AM|||| Time to put a Fiero spin on this post.<BR>So why is it, that these this "saftey-device<BR>I'am required to wear while driving the car<BR>has been discontinued. <P>(Playing devil''s advocate here) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>If it is truely that important, should it not be equally important that they are in good/safe condition and replacments are readily available.<BR>Afterall if there is a SAFTEY recall on a vehicle,it does have to be honored no matter<BR>how old the vehicle is.<BR>(No expiration date there) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P><BR>With me so far?<BR>Then get this,why would the goverment department for saftey/bla..bla..bla<BR>direct me to the junk-yard for a replacment of my seatbelts?<P>Tina<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Songman||04-29-2001||12:58 AM||||Touche', Tina...<P>Mach10... you don't need a flame suit from anything I will say to you... but the gov't might just make you wear one anyway.... heheh<P>I'll make my point one more time and then I am done with this... YOU have the right to your opinion, but I do too.... I won't try to force mine on you and I'll thank you to give me the same respect. But that is what all these little special interests groups do. And I don't care what gov't you are representing, or what faction, or what anything... that is just wrong. <P>Forget governments and countries and just talk about people. Everyone deserves to live their life the way they want to. You can spout off facts and figures all day long. I'll guarantee you that the other side can give you just as many and they will both be just as true. My point is, and always has been... let me say it one more time for the people who miss it.... you or no one else has the right to tell me that I can't do something because I might get hurt, or that I have to do this to keep me from getting hurt. It is MY business and it effects you or them in no way.<P>And you know what? I think me and every other American here will say that we don't really give a $@*# what the rest of the world thinks. When the time comes for us to stand together we will do just that... And I am not saying that America is above anyone, or a heartless nation.. I am just saying that so many people that aren't a part of this country always get off on making such statements.... Let's check the immigration numbers for Canada and compare that to the US. I don't see that many people trying to get into Canada....<P>And you see, now you have made me sound anti-Canadian, which I am not at all... Just don't sit way up there in the north and judge my country. I love my country, but there is a lot I don't love about the political system, but it still tops anything else the world has to offer...<P>Amazing that all this comes because some people want to make the whole world wear seatbelts... hehehe<P>I love all you guys anyway and still enjoy being a part of this forum.... heheh<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Spektyr||04-29-2001||01:28 AM||||Well those that know me know that I can rarely resist the opportunity to put my 2 cents worth in.  So here goes...<P>I <B>always</B> wear my seatbelt.  Just today I'm moved my cars around.  Just changed where they were parked out front of my house.  Wore seatbelts in both cars.<P>Why?<P>Because I <I>cannot drive</I> without a seatbelt on.  Really.  I have worn a seatbelt while driving everytime I've been behind the wheel.  I simply do not feel comfortable without it on.  My first few cars had front bench seats, and I have always liked to test my car's limits.  Without the seatbelt I'd have put dents in my roof or ended up driving from the passenger side a few times.  I like to <B>drive</B> my cars.<P>In my Fiero the seatbelt is increasingly important because I do not like showering with broken glass.  I'm 6'4".  Without the seatbelt if I hit an unexpected and large bump I'd put my head through the sunroof (only about 3" of clearance).  Heck, I wish 5-point harnesses weren't so expensive.<P>While it would suck to be trapped in a car with a seatbelt that won't release, that's why I like to carry a large serrated knife.  And there really isn't anywhere for me to duck if I'm going to take the top of the car off going under a semi.<P>MOST of the time a seatbelt will save your life.  SOMEtimes it will get you killed.  But hey, driving is the act of piloting a 1 ton or more vehicle at high speed.  Duh, you can get hurt doing that.<P>Should we be required to wear seatbelts?  I think if you're old enough to make your own decisions that should be one you get to make.  For yourself only.  And in my opinion that includes kids.  The default setting should be to play the odds, which say you're more likely to live wearing the seatbelt.<P>So where I stand is this:  Legal adults should get to decide to wear them or not.  Children should be required to wear them simply because they can't make a legally binding decision, and I don't think parents should get to make a decision that statistically reduces that child's chance of surviving an accident.<P>On the other hand... if you should (god forbid) be in an accident that is ruled your fault and the occupant(s) of the other car are seriously injured or killed because they weren't wearing their seatbelts under our current law you are liable for their hospital/death costs.  And their families can sue you.  So if it's made legal to not wear a seatbelt, they need to make that also carry an implied acceptance of a maximum liability on the other driver's part.<P>Otherwise insurance companies will have to raise their premiums even more to cover drivers in case the occupants of the other car suffer additional injuries due to not wearing their seatbelts.  (Because we all know they won't be happy unless they're making hundreds of millions of dollars a year.)<P>Additionally, I do require everyone in a car I'm driving to wear their seatbelts.  Why?  Two reasons.  First, I can get ticketed if they aren't.  Second, if I wreck the car and they died because they weren't wearing the seatbelt, I don't need the emotional baggage of constantly questioning myself about why I didn't insist they wear it.<P>Finally, I'd guess that even if you live in the most Nazi-seatbelt-wearing state you won't be carted off to jail for not wearing your seatbelt unless there is some other reason to warrant it.  Think about it... the cop out there pulling you over probably doesn't want to do all the paperwork to book you for not wearing your seatbelt.  Now if you show him some attitude, don't have proof of insurance or registration, or do something to raise his suspicion... yeah, he might take you in hoping something bigger will turn up.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Shaft||04-29-2001||01:38 AM||||A few years ago I was doing an internship in Columbus. Part of the internship was to do weekly "educational" sessions. In one of these sessions were had to speak to a member of the Ohio Gen. Assemly on the mandatory seatbelt law the was going through the house at that time. Luckly I was assigned to speak against the law. I used many arguements including the one theogre used on here. I brought pictures and brochures on new cars that illustrated my points. He was very impressed by my speach and seemed genuinely interested in what I had to say. Some people there suggested that I should come back and give the speech duing an actual house session, I told them to call me to set it up, but nothing happened. (He had all my materials though) Sadly, I think this bill has passed in Ohio. It will just be used as a tool for police profiling and harassment. Now a police officer can pull you over for "suspicion" of not wearing a seat belt. Which to me means when ever he damn well pleases.<P>Yes, I wear my seatbelt all the time and I beleive they help save lives. But when it's an issue of PERSONAL safety it should be left up o the individual. Being in a free country I do not feel it it the governments place to tell me how to make a decision that effects ONLY me.||||reg||1
Z||000020||rogergarrison||04-29-2001||06:15 AM||||Im waiting now for the government mandate that all cars have to be made with complete roll cages/ crash bars. Shouldnt add much cost since most cars now are in the over $30K price range anyway and they have been proven to be far safer in a crash up to 200mph. Police will carry a device to measure tube wall thickness and you may be asked to pull over at any time for a safety bar check if there is any suspicion that your vehicle is not legal.||||reg||1
Z||000021||SteveJ||04-29-2001||09:08 AM||||I'd love a roll cage. They make those cars like paper mache. Ever see those pictures of the Insurance Institute crashing cars and the legs get crushed or severed? Manufacturer negligence.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Songman||04-29-2001||10:13 AM||||One step farther on the police being able to do whatever they want... I'm not sure if it is just Tennessee or nationwide but now the police can make you take a breathalizer test. Up until now, of course, you could refuse, but that refusal was basically considered admitting you were guilty. Now they can just pull anyone over and give them a test for no reason. Of course, everyone will say that they will have to have reason for suspicion. Is that like everytime I have ever driven through Ohio and gotten pulled over because I am a long haired guy in a Porsche. A guy up there actually searched my car once! What was my crime? I was pulling out of Wendy's as he was pulling in for lunch. He followed me a mile and pulled me over and searched my car. No ticket, no reason for suspicion that I can see other than the way I looked.... And no, I wasn't wearing a seat belt then either...<P>As people say, to put a Fiero twist on this... I got pulled over the othre day in my new T-top. Second day and I already got a ticket! Motorcycle cop got me doing 70 in a 55. I don't know why the speed limit was 55 on a stretch of interstate with 5 lanes in each direction, but it was. Anyway, after he had to ask me what kind of car I was driving so he could write the ticket, I signed it and was on my way... He never even mentioned the fact that I was not wearing a seat belt. Luckily, not all people in authority even agree with the laws. Maybe it is because he was a biker. I don't know...<P>Anyway.. still loving the T-top. <P>&lt;Close of Fiero Story&gt;<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mach10||04-29-2001||12:43 PM||||Songman: *grin* <BR>No hard feelings. It *IS* a debate after all   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I'm NOT trying to force my opinion on anyone. But I do love a good debate   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I would like it to be known that yes, there is movement south of Canada, but it only involves the government in terms of taxation and wages. CA's high-tech industry sucks. Our taxes bite ass. For a low-middle income bracket, we lose 16% off pay cheque (NOT including other decucts) and pay a further 7-14% on ANY purchase with whatever we have left over. With huge US firms making attractive offers down south, who can blame 'em? If I had stayed in CompSci, *I* probably would have moved south   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I like my country too, and am quite happy to stay here. Judging is human nature, and I will continue to do so, but understand that I'm not "anti-american." I make the same arguments towards my government. I'm not targetting the US all the time. As a matter of fact, I do have several relatives down there, and get to see some of the absurdity first hand, but I also see a lot of really cool things that SHOULD be happening up here. I don't think that any one country is better than the other. To believe so is being ethnocentric. Every country that I've lived in has ups and downs. Canada: Taxation. UK: Class stratification. Spain: Absurd property values, militant policing(ever been pulled over by a cop carrying a sub-machine gun?). But I digress....<P>I will concede to your point, songman. I still believe that the government has a responsibility towards it's people, but I guess it could be better used by education and promotional programs, rather than a jail sentence. After some thought, I will agree that getting a jail sentence 'cos you unbuckled for a moment to grab that coffee cup you left on the seat that now fell on the floor and is leaking on your upholstery is kinda silly.<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 04-29-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000024||Songman||04-30-2001||01:19 AM||||I am glad that you understood that I wasn't trying to be some kind of hard-ass. I was worried about that today as I was on my way to Virginia... There was one line that kept popping into my head that I wished I had closed my last post with...<P>As much as I value my freedom to my own opinions and choices, I would also fight right beside you to make sure you have the same...<P>And for me, that is the bottom line. No one deserves less. And I liked the coffee on the upholstery thing! heheh<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000025||JSocha||04-30-2001||10:02 AM||||Let me relay something:<P>I've been in two "serious" accidents within the past 10 years.<P>In 1991, I rolled my '88 Chrysler LeBaron once on a gravel road and ended up "outside" the vehicle.<P>The Highway patrol officer who responded to the accident made the following comment to me among others:<P>"Good thing you weren't wearing your seatbelt or you would have been crushed under the vehicle instead of thrown out like you were."<P>Response:  "I was wearing my seatbelt."<P>I had the officer inspect the vehicle and the seatbelt was still "in fact" fastened.<P>Now, if anybody is familiar with the 87 & 88 LeBaron's, you will realize that there is a Shoulder belt that attaches to the door post with a separate lap stap.<P>Somehow in the accident, I slipped out through the belt and went out the vehicles sunroof when it was rolling.  We think that my sister in law, while in the process of rolling as I grabbed her and pulled her down on the center council, she grabbed my belt and loosened it enough for me to slip out of which "saved" my life according to the officer.<P>The officer commented had that not happened I would have been dead for sure...now this is coming from someone that is supposed to enforce the "seat belt" law here in ND.<P>My second accident happened in May of 97 with my 97 Dodge 1-ton my wife and I just bought after a KISS concert in Fargo.  With under 700 miles on the vehicle and 5 days old, we were heading home on our lunch break to let our dogs out when we ran into the back tire of a tractor when the tractor moved into the lane we were now passing him on.  This time I wasn't wearing my seatbelt and I walked away without a scratch (shaken and pissed of course).  My wife however, who was wearing her seatbelt was injured and taken to the hospital.<P>It's a gamble either way.  It's like your chances of being hit by lightning.<P>I have many friends on the EMT who go to EMT conventions where speakers talk about the use of seatbelts and supposedly where they have never pulled someone "dead" out of vehicle where an individual has worn their seatbelts.<P>Fact:  Back in 1986 I lost a classmate to a vehicle accident.  She was wearing her seatbelt and was killed because she couldn't escape from the vehicle.  Another friend drowned when her and her friend went off a bridge up at a lake.  The EMT's themselves could not get the belt undone after they pulled the vehicle out of the water and ended up cutting the belt to remove my friend.<P>As "SONGMAN" stated, it is a choice, that which is personal in nature and that we ourselves should be able to decide not some "big brother" as we call our government.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mach10||05-01-2001||02:31 AM||||Songman: You don't deal with too many Canucks, do you?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Most of us will tolerate about anything... <P>Personally, as long as it wasn't malicious, I just pass it off as "part of the argument" and leave it at that. This means that while I may mercilessly man-beat you in one thread, I'll back you in another (or back down  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). People who get personally involved in a political debate (such as this) are taking themselves way too seriously. <BR>Oh, and while some of my posts may be considered inflammatory, consider that I think my arguments through fairly carefully, and will cheerfully correct any mistakes I make. If you hate my opinion, feel free to say so. Just think your rebuttals through. You don't want an ugly flame war starting.<BR>Actually, I miss flame-wars. I have a whole BAG of really cool insults  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
